    28	
    29	He had come down to the ground floor for a candle. The guesting-house had a parlour at the back, a square room with a long table where the factors settled their accounts, and its door had two panes set in the upper half. The lamp was lit inside, and he would have gone past if a small bright thing on the table had not moved, and ticked.
    30	
    31	It was the steel rule.
    32	
    33	The counsel sat at the table's end with the code open in front of her, the fat volume with the schedules, and a single sheet beside it that was not the house's paper. Withrow sat across from her with both hands on the wood. Bracken stood by the hearth with his back to the room. His spectacles were pushed up on his forehead and he held a slip of paper in his hand, and he looked like a man who had read something off a wall and wished he had not been the one to walk past it.
    34	
    35	The counsel was speaking. Cael could not hear a word. He saw her lay one finger on a line in the code and then on the sheet, and then on the code again, lower down, as a person does who is adding days. He saw the rule turn its quarter turn at the end of it.
    36	
    37	Withrow said something short, and the counsel answered, and then for a long moment nobody in that room spoke at all.
    38	
