    50	
    51	Cael came out onto the gallery with the satchel on his shoulder and looked down. The yard was flagged, with a pump in one corner and a stack of empty woolsacks under a pentice roof. In the middle stood the press, a thing as old as the building: two oak posts as thick as a man, a beam across their tops, and a great wooden screw coming down through the beam to a platen of black timber six feet square. A capstan with four long bars turned the screw. In sale weeks, the woman of the house had said, it took four men to bale a sack with it. It was not a sale week.
    52	
    53	Brom stood under the platen.
    54	
    55	He had his coat off and his feet set and his forearms crossed above his head, the right over the left, and the platen rested on them. Lira was at the capstan with both hands on a bar and her heels dug in, walking it round a quarter turn at a time. With every quarter the black timber came down a finger's breadth, and Brom took it.
    56	
    57	"More," said Brom.
    58	
    59	"That's all of me. I weigh what I weigh."
    60	
