    72	
    73	The bar dropped under her and the screw turned, and the platen came down on Brom's crossed forearms like the lid of a chest.
    74	
    75	His knees gave an inch, and held.
    76	
    77	Cael had let the old read go out across the flags without deciding to. It came back to him as it always did, in weight and where the weight went. The platen's whole mass, and a girl's, and the speed she had brought with her, ran down through two forearms into the shoulders, both alike now, and down the long back and through the heels into the stone. The skin had gone dull at the wrists. Brom stood under a wool press like a third post.
    78	
    79	Lira was standing on the capstan bar on one foot with her arms out, perfectly balanced, grinning.
    80	
    81	"*That's* a little over," she said.
    82	
    83	"Again," said Brom.
    84	
    85	They did it nine times. She had four free bursts on stone in a day, and she spent them, and after that she ran at the bar and jumped, which was not the same and was near enough. She took it on the right foot and then on the left, and once, for nobody's benefit that Cael could see, on the right with the left already swinging through for the step off. The bar was round and moving and wet, and she landed on it every time as she would have landed on a chalk mark. He thought of a girl in the east hall in the recess, touching one foot down and stepping to the other. He did not think she remembered it herself.
    86	
    87	On the ninth Brom said, "Enough," and she walked the capstan back, and the platen rose off him, and the skin went back to being skin.
    88	
    89	He sat down on the pump trough with his forearms on his knees. There were two red bars across them.
    90	
    91	"Three days," said Lira. She had hitched herself up onto a capstan bar and was swinging her feet. "And then three people in a draughty hall write *Iron* on you, and you're a rank nobody can take off."
    92	
    93	"They'll find it."
    94	
    95	"You say that as if it were bad weather."
    96	
    97	"I say it like I say anything." Brom turned his forearms over and looked at the marks. "I'll stand in it, and I'll hold what they set, and the plate will say what's so. I want that. I've wanted it since before I had a Path." He was quiet a moment. "It's the paper after that I've stopped being able to see properly. Somebody hands you a sheet. It says *found*, and there's a line at the bottom for your name."
    98	
    99	Lira stopped swinging her feet.
