   118	
   119	It was true, every word. He read it over once and signed it and dated it for the morning, and laid it on top of the kit, where he would see it when he woke, to hand in at the district seat on his way to the exchange.
   120	
   121	The bridge-ward had lit his lantern. Havel sat at the window with his aching hand open on his knee and watched it, and thought, a little, about the young man at the far table, who had sat up straight for a year. He did not know what a ruling would do to him. He had never known what any of it would do; that had not been his to know. He found, sitting there, that he hoped the registers would be kind to him. He knew of no register that entered kindness, and he hoped it all the same.
   122	
   123	Then the lantern went out, and he went to bed.
