   160	
   161	The long room held more than it had ever held, and nobody in it made a sound.
   162	
   163	They were standing along the walls, and the doors had been left open so that the steps could hear, and the steps were full, and past them the street. Everybody who had come down for the closing had come again, and Ephram's cohort had the ninth and tenth benches, and this time nobody had needed to threaten them.
   164	
   165	Cael sat behind Withrow's chair with lot seventeen under his boot, and he sat up straight. It was, as the counsel had told him once, nearly the last time he would have to.
