   206	The town went first. It went up the tiers and out by the yard gate in a slow loud river, wool-factors and carters and clerks, and he heard his name in it, the registry's and the hill's, and a good many guesses, and let them go by. The lake house's Blade came down to the rim before he left and stood looking at the oak for some time with his lath across his shoulders; then he looked at Cael, and laughed, and shook his head, and went away.
   207	
   208	The house went more slowly, for it did not seem to know how to leave. First-years stood about in knots on the tiers, re-fighting the fourth exchange with their hands. Nobody came down the steps to him, and that was Lira's doing, who had put herself at the head of the east flight with her arms folded.
   209	
   210	Withrow was the last.
   211	
   212	She had not left the registrar's chair. She sat in it, upright, with her hands in her lap, while the stand emptied round her and Bracken went down with the house's book under his arm, and she went on sitting until the tiers were bare stone again and the porter had begun at the top with his broom. Then she stood.
   213	
