   215	
   216	Her face was not the registry face, and he did not know what it was. He thought that she had seen a thing on her own oak that explained two years of an enrollee looking ordinary, and that she was adding it, quite calmly, to the things she had decided not to ask about.
   217	
   218	She inclined her head to him. It was not the registry's depth; it was a good deal less, and it was meant.
   219	
   220	Then she went out by the north arch, alone, and she was the last out of the yard.
   221	
   222	On the tier above him the Current girl had come back for a scarf. She stopped beside Lira, red-eyed and hoarse.
   223	
   224	"I've trained on this hill with him for two years," she said. "I've watched every wing-three card there was. I never saw one bit of that. Not one." She wiped her nose on her wrist. "Where's he been *keeping* it?"
   225	
   226	Lira did not answer the question. She looked down the steps at him, and then out over the empty oak.
   227	
   228	"That's the first time this school has ever seen him," she said.
   229	
   230	---
   231	
   232	Ephram came back when the light had begun to go yellow.
   233	
   234	He came down the steps slowly, with his right arm bound across his chest in a sling of the healer's linen and his coat hung over the shoulder. Rooke's clerk was waiting by the boards with the chalk and the bout sheet, and Cael got up off his step and went along the stand to them.
   235	
   236	It was the yard's custom, and he had watched it from a tier a hundred times and never stood in it: after a bout on the oak the clerk chalked the result, and the two who had fought stood at the board while he did. The first board was high. The clerk set the porter's ladder against it, and Cael steadied one side of it with his one good hand and Ephram the other with his, and neither of them said that it wanted steadying.
   237	
   238	The clerk chalked the result under the notice in the same three-line grammar the yard used for every bout. Cael and Ephram stood together while he did it. Ephram looked at the board rather than at Cael, which was the register the ritual ran in.
   239	
   240	"Whatever you are," he said, "I said I'm glad it's on my side of the floor. At the trial. When she stopped to read you." He looked at his bound shoulder. "The floor's about to be the whole continent, and I'm not on it — I'm staying; you know I'm staying; it's the right thing for me and you'd tell me so if I asked, which is why I'm not asking." He put out his left hand. "Still glad. That's the whole thing. I made myself wait two years to fight you straight and it was worth every month, and I don't need to know what you are to know that. Go be it somewhere with more room."
   241	
   242	Cael took the offered hand with his right, which was the only one he could close, and for a moment neither of them let go. The clerk came down the ladder beside them and went away with his sheet.
   243	
   244	It was in him, new, housed for the moment where nothing else was, a thing with an edge and a rule to it. It had come out of the man in front of him an hour ago, and he could have said so in a dozen words. *There's something I took. You should know what you gave.*
   245	
   246	Ephram would have listened. He would have gone still, and thought about it, and very probably said that he was glad of that as well. But he had just said, of his own accord, that he did not need to know; he had turned his back on a table of papers twice, for a friend's sake, on the same understanding.
   247	
   248	And then he would have known. He would have been a cohort captain at a chartered house who knew what the registry's unclassified enrollee was, and what he had carried east, and he would have known it on the day a district officer rode up to the gate, and for every day after. He was staying. He had a board with his name on it, and they would come and ask him, courteously, what he had seen.
   249	
   250	Cael did not say it.
   251	
   252	"You've a look on you," said Ephram.
   253	
