editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:55:"I should like to begin by reading the seat a list. It is the list of what is agreed between the parties, and it is the longest such list I have found in any contested proceeding in the digests. I looked, because I wanted to be able to say so truthfully." She did not look down, for there was nothing to look down at. "That the enrollment was lawfully executed. That the provision it was executed under is law. That the renewals are good upon their days. That the evaluator of record is competent in every particular. That every figure he has signed was honestly come by. That the tournament office's record is accurate. That the house has kept within its charter. Seven things. The seat held the first two at the preliminary, and the petitioner gave me the other five himself, across three sittings, without being asked twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:111:"And the fourth," said Karis, and her little finger went down. "I asked. I asked the petitioner what evidence would defeat his petition, and a reading in the frame is evidence, and he told me there was none. He did not say, *let us see what the frame returns.* He is too honest a man to say so, and in thirteen sittings he never has. Nobody has. No officer, no clerk, no witness has stood in this room and told the seat that the frame will return a classification. It was asked once before, at a district station, when he was fourteen, and it took eleven seconds to answer, and its answer is the word on his file."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:127:"Now I come back to the handle. These sections are for the testing of instruments, so that the registry may know which of them to trust, and there are two instruments before the seat. One is a frame, which was asked about this practitioner once and had no answer. The other is twenty-two officers of the registry. The house's counsel read their names to the seat at the preliminary, each with a grade and a house and a day: a provost's panel, a regional registrar's assessors, the tournament's, an evaluator of record who has sat across a trestle from him every week for two years. They watched, and wrote down what they saw, and signed it. Their notes agree with one another, and the petitioner has accepted every one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:201:"*Evaluation-protocol certification: declined by the evaluator of record; proceeding on the presiding seat's authority.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:205:It was a small thing to see, and he did not think the benches saw it. The four district evaluators had been writing in a row, and at that line they stopped, one after another, like men on a road who see the one ahead halt. They were working officers. Every one of them had stood practitioners in frames for years, under a protocol that carried the Archmarshal's certificate as a matter of course, as a loaf carries a baker's mark; the certifying office had put its name to that protocol since before any of them was born. It had been asked twice for its name and had not given it, and the form had no line for why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:209:On the fourth bench Gault had not moved. He was the evaluator of record, the only man in that room who was called so every day, and he had declined nothing in his life. He sat with his case straight on his knees and his eyes on the pasted form, and then he took out his small book and wrote a line in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:99:It was a form. He had drawn it from the district seat's stationery press some weeks ago, on the day after the eleventh sitting, and had carried it blank ever since: *Request for relief from rotation*, a single leaf with five ruled heads. He had never filled one in. In all his service he had gone where he was sent and stayed until he was fetched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:107:*Posting sought.* The district seat's records room was a man short. He had seen it for himself when he went there with a rubbing of a seal: an impression book four months behind, and a ledger of dies that nobody had balanced against the cutters' copies since the last officer left. He wrote *District records, Ostrand, or any records room that is short.* He did not write *out of monitoring*, for the form had no line for what a man wished to leave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:119:It was true, every word. He read it over once and signed it and dated it for the morning, and laid it on top of the kit, where he would see it when he woke, to hand in at the district seat on his way to the exchange.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:263:*I keep looking for the person and finding the hand.* That was his own line, from the year's sixth week, and he had gone on looking, at Jent and at a hood and down a road with chalk on it. He stopped looking. It was like setting down a thing he had carried so long that he had forgotten it had weight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:35:"I stand in it. Within five weeks, at the district station, with Ilsev seeing to the conduct of it, and she'll see to it properly. I put my hand where I'm shown. It was asked once before, and it took eleven seconds." He did not look at Seln, and he went no nearer to it than that. "We know what it says. Karis told a seat so for fifty-one minutes and nobody contradicted her: *no valid classification.* Then the chapter opens, in its order. One: every enrollment or standing that rests on my being a practitioner is void, and that's this hill. Two: I'm suspended as a practitioner pending a review, and the review is to be begun by the registry at its discretion."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:67:"I'm arguing. Let me." Karis held up a finger. "It needn't be the convening. There's the Archmarshal's office. He has read it. He stood at a window in a borrowed room and said what it was, and he has since refused the protocol his name, and a finding from him, with the case behind it, is a thing no seat could set aside as a forgery. And if not him, there's the press. There's a man in the market street who bought a second press this year on the strength of our name, and a Gold at Auremont who has already told the registry in public to classify its instruments. Forty families could be told what was done to their children. It's true, and it's proved, and in my hands it's dated." Her voice had risen a little. "I have never in my life held a true thing and not filed it. I don't know how. Everything I am says that you put it in front of somebody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:307:"I told you at the trial that whatever you are, I was glad it was on my side of the floor," said Ephram. "I said some hard things on a tier in your first months here, and I've been glad every day since that I was wrong. I'd like to find out whether I'm still glad when you stop being careful with me. I think I will be. I'd like to know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:85:They did it nine times. She had four free bursts on stone in a day, and she spent them, and after that she ran at the bar and jumped, which was not the same and was near enough. She took it on the right foot and then on the left, and once, for nobody's benefit that Cael could see, on the right with the left already swinging through for the step off. The bar was round and moving and wet, and she landed on it every time as she would have landed on a chalk mark. He thought of a girl in the east hall in the recess, touching one foot down and stepping to the other. He did not think she remembered it herself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:123:"It's upon the entry they read out," said Karis, as they crossed the lane. "The certificate. The code lets a seat go on by its own authority where the certificate's withheld; I looked at the section the night we got home. So I went back through the digests to see how often a seat ever had." She held up four fingers. "Four times. I've set them out, with their years. A guild hall's frame, where the assessor wouldn't sign. Two station frames in the hill districts. A plate. Four times in the whole record a seat has proceeded without the certifying office's name, and every time it was upon a thing. Never once upon a person."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:149:The north road left the town between tanyards and came out among garden plots and low walls. A little short of the toll-bar, on the left, stood the district station.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:153:It was a low stone house with a slate roof, and behind it a second building, windowless, with a wide door, which was the frame-house. Over the front door a sigil was cut in the lintel. There was a bench under the wall, in the sun, and a board beside the door with the day's hours chalked on it in a round hand. It was the most ordinary building he had ever seen. There was one like it at the edge of every town on the continent that had a registry, and he had not stood in front of one since Denvash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:163:He knew what was in that building. He knew it as he knew the inside of Hesk's shop. There was a room with a brass plate in it. The girl would be told to stand still, and to put her hand where she was shown, and something in her that had been asleep for fourteen years would wake, and the plate would light. In the frame-house a keeper would read what the frame gave him, and write it in his station book in two figures, a reading and a margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:205:*Third-day. The district station, the north road. A girl of fourteen. Her mother had done her hair.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:45:"The enrollment was lawfully made. The provision under which it was made is law, and has not been repealed, and the seat has held so. The renewals are good upon their days; the respondent's table of them is the petitioner's own. The evaluator of record is competent, and his figures are honest, and his notes were produced entire. The tournament office's record is accepted. And the house has kept within its charter in every particular I have been able to examine, and I examined a great many." He paused. "I want one of those in the minute in my own voice, and not only in a schedule. The Chancellor has done nothing wrong. She answered under escort. She read every order at her own gate. She produced what was asked before it fell due. If every respondent I met were the Chancellor, I should have very little work, and I should think the better of the registry for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:172:"Both. Enter both."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:236:It was the yard's custom, and he had watched it from a tier a hundred times and never stood in it: after a bout on the oak the clerk chalked the result, and the two who had fought stood at the board while he did. The first board was high. The clerk set the porter's ladder against it, and Cael steadied one side of it with his one good hand and Ephram the other with his, and neither of them said that it wanted steadying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:238:The clerk chalked the result under the notice in the same three-line grammar the yard used for every bout. Cael and Ephram stood together while he did it. Ephram looked at the board rather than at Cael, which was the register the ritual ran in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:240:"Whatever you are," he said, "I said I'm glad it's on my side of the floor. At the trial. When she stopped to read you." He looked at his bound shoulder. "The floor's about to be the whole continent, and I'm not on it — I'm staying; you know I'm staying; it's the right thing for me and you'd tell me so if I asked, which is why I'm not asking." He put out his left hand. "Still glad. That's the whole thing. I made myself wait two years to fight you straight and it was worth every month, and I don't need to know what you are to know that. Go be it somewhere with more room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:264:*Nine. Taken in earnest from a friend who asked me to show him straight — so I showed him straight, and the architecture did what it does with earnest, and the notice said "non-hostile," which it has never said, because there has never been a fight like it in the log. Ephram asked for the true measurement. He got it. He doesn't know he gave me a piece of himself for it; he consented to a fight, not to the thing the fight fed. I wrote the price down before the bout, the way the covenant says, so that when it cost — and it cost — I'd have written the cost before I collected it, which is the only line between me and what the office did at a road cut and in a records hall — spending people whose names it never bothered to write down, and asking nobody anything.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:31:"The ruling says five weeks," she said. "That is the outside of it. The inside is in a section upon notices, which gives a district five days, and in the habits of a district clerk who has never in my time notified a date one day later than the code allowed him. Bracken went to the district hall on our first morning in the town, to look at the room where Brom would stand, and there is a board beside its door. He read on it that the station's frame-house was reserved for the registry's use on the last day of the year." Her voice was quite even. "It was written up before any ruling was given, and I want you to understand that there was nothing crooked in that. A prudent clerk books a frame when he sees that one may be wanted, exactly as Bracken books a coach, and he would have struck it off again without a thought if the seat had gone the other way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:35:"It is five days. An officer will ride up to my gate with the notice tomorrow, or the day after, and it will be addressed as the last one was: to the chancellor, to produce the enrollee. You will still be mine on that day, for the revocation does not bite until its close." She looked down at the four lines. "I read an order aloud at that gate once and declined it, because nobody could tell me whose it was. This one would be a seat's, and I could not decline it. I should have to walk you into that frame-house myself, under the house's seal, or else refuse a seat in writing and let them enter the refusal in my folder."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:53:"So I kept it," she said. "For four days, through a return and a ruling. I let you hear the hood say *revoked* without knowing how short the time was. I should like to say that it was easy and that I never wavered, and I can't; I sat in a carriage this afternoon and very nearly said it through the window." Her mouth moved a little. "I wanted it to be yours. If you went, I wanted you to go because you had priced it with your own people, at a table, with my need left out of the sum. And if you stayed, I meant to walk beside you into that frame-house and take what they wrote."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:185:He had not waited for the porter, or for morning. Somebody had come to his door at midnight and told him, and he had put on his coat and gone down to the yard in the dark and chalked it himself, before any officer of the house could wake up and wonder whether it was wise. She watched until he climbed down and held the lantern up to read it over, and then rubbed out one word with his cuff and wrote it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:283:He stood on the north chalk with his feet together. He turned the lath over once in his hand, and set its tip on the oak, and folded both hands on the grip, as a man leans on a spade. The yard waited. Rooke's hand had come down, and the glass was turned, and the sand was running.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:321:He had done it on a stone floor at Norhold with eight thousand people watching, and on a chalk road, and in a painted hall at night, and he had never done it here. He set his feet and asked, and a lane went down across the Crown yard from his chalk to the space behind Ephram's left shoulder, fifteen feet of it, shoulder-wide. His ears closed and opened. The dust in the joints of the oak woke and ran, pale, along the line.
