   289	Two Silvers had filed. There would not be a third from anybody who still had a tier to lose.
   290	
   291	---
   292	
   293	Rooke stopped them in the tunnel.
   294	
   295	It was half dark under the north tiers, and the roar of the bowl came down through the stone over their heads as a long dull thunder. He stood in their way with his coat over his arm and waited until all six of them, the five and the white-faced reserve, had stopped walking and were looking at him.
   296	
   297	"Before anybody says a word to anybody," said Rooke. "The third exchange wasn't a squad problem."
   298	
   299	Nobody said anything.
   300	
   301	"I want that understood before you get to the stair, because by the stair you'll all have started blaming yourselves, and I won't have it." He looked from one of them to the next. "Nothing a squad does answers the third. Nothing a bracket fighter does, either. I've stood at a great many rails, and I've never once seen one fighter take a whole scenario floor away from nine others, and nobody else in this building has either. Every coach up there is writing that down right now in his own hand." He shifted the coat on his arm. "So nobody reviews it. Not you, and not me. The first two, and the fourth, we'll take apart tonight, and you'll be proud of every piece. The third goes in the file as weather."
   302	
   303	He stood aside, and they went past him up the tunnel toward the light.
   304	
   305	That night he did not hold a review at all, not in the way he usually held one. He passed his coaching sheet round the long table instead, face up, with a pencil, and told each of them to write one line on it, their own finding, and pass it on.
   306	
   307	Lira wrote *Ready. Not early.* and passed it to Brom.
   308	
   309	Brom took a long time over his. He wrote *Left the gap. Should have left it sooner.* and looked at it, and pushed the sheet on to Karis without a word.
   310	
   311	Karis wrote *A frame with a door in it,* very small, and then, under that, *She came from where no ramp was. Think about that.* She gave it to Ephram.
   312	
   313	Ephram wrote *South.* and nothing else, and went red, and passed it to Cael.
   314	
   315	Cael held the pencil over the sheet for a while. Then he wrote: *The diagonal. I had it on paper this morning and didn't know what it was.* He gave the sheet back to Rooke.
   316	
   317	Rooke read all five lines, slowly, at the head of the table. He did not say anything about any of them. He folded the sheet into the coaching file, and closed the file, and sat with his hand flat on the cover of it for a moment longer than he needed to.
   318	
   319	"That'll do," he said. "That'll do very well. Eat something."
   320	
   321	---
   322	
   323	Ephram found him later in the courtyard, by the pump, where Cael had gone down to put his right hand under the cold water, though it no longer needed it.
   324	
   325	Ephram did not say anything at first. He worked the pump handle for him, three long strokes, and stood back, and waited until Cael had dried his hand on his shirt.
   326	
   327	"At Halcenvane," he said, "I told myself I was going to be careful how I remembered it. All of it. You, especially." He looked at the water running away across the cobbles. "I have been careful. I've watched you from the rail for a year, and I've waited for a floor where I'd see it for myself, whatever it is, with nobody telling me what I was looking at. Today I was on the floor." He looked up. "And she was ahead. She had the exchange, and the floor, and the whole bowl on its feet for her, and she gave up a whole second of it to stand there and look at you. Like we look at you. Like you look at everything."
   328	
   329	He put out his hand.
   330	
   331	"I'm done waiting. Whatever you are, I'm glad it's on my side of the floor."
   332	
   333	Cael took the hand and shook it.
   334	
   335	Ephram let go and stood back, looking faintly embarrassed, as he always did when he had said something he meant.
   336	
   337	"I wrote most of that on the road to the mill town," he said. "It's been in my file book ever since, waiting for an afternoon to go with it. It had four more sentences and Lira cut them."
   338	
   339	"When did Lira see it?"
   340	
   341	"At the waystation. I asked her to." Ephram considered. "She said it was a good speech with a sermon stuck on the end."
   342	
   343	The third entry in Hesk's book went under the inventory and the line about tomorrow.
   344	
   345	*Team trial, semifinal. Two exchanges each, and the ledger to Auremont on the third. For two of the four, the five of us were the best squad in the building, and the marshals counted it, and it's in their book now with ours. Last night I wrote down what the tournament was for, and today it turned out to be true.*
   346	
   347	*And the third exchange was hers.*
   348	
   349	*She stopped mid-trial to read me. Gold Rank 3, nineteen years old, and the first person at this tournament whose attention feels like mine. Filed under: things that are going to happen whether I schedule them or not.*
   350	
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:51:In the back room Brom had his kit open on the bed and was packing it by a system nobody else understood, the shirts rolled as tight as bandages. The plaque went in last, in its own two shirts, with a letter of one line laid against the back of it. Lira was on the other bed with her boots off. She asked Brom whether he meant to think about the district seat yet, since his summons would be lying there waiting; the notice had said so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:141:On the ninth day they reached the district seat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:111:The committee room had a long table and a portrait of a dead miller over the fireplace, and the panel sat along one side of the table with their backs to the portrait. It was the panel of record Gault had written into his calendar at the end of last term, in his ruled lines: *Gault, with two.* The two were a registry officer from the district seat, a careful dark man who had plainly read the whole file in the coach on the way and had the page numbers ready, and the host region's senior examiner, a stout grey woman who had sat on the meet's own panels all week and who said very little.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:141:The officer from the district seat initialled the minute. The examiner initialled it. Gault initialled it last, and pressed the seal, and wrote the one word under it that the provision required.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:157:*At the end the man from the district seat said "routine," and Gault said "routine" back, and they shook hands on it. Not long ago a file like mine went upstairs at a run. Today it's a word two officers trade over a sealed minute while a christening waits on the stair.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:33:It was a registry notice. It said, in the registry's careful language, that a continental bracket title at any tier required a mandatory review of the titled practitioner's classification, and that the review was hereby noticed. The summons proper would issue from the registry's district seat, to be collected there by the practitioner or his house upon the delegation's return. Until the review was held, the practitioner's classification stood as entered.

