editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:5:It was the only use he could find for the quarter hour before the seat came in. The coach had gone down the bluff road at the usual pace behind Withrow's carriage, and the two coats at the road's foot had looked at it as they looked at it every Fifth-day, and the young officer at the side door had written the same two lines in his log. But the exchange's four steps had been crowded to the plinth this morning, and the benches behind Cael were filling with a noise he had not heard in that room since the preliminary. A closing, the house's counsel had said, was the one day besides the first when a proceeding sold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:23:The clerks in their grey had come to see their own seat do its work. The correspondents would write down what was said, and get some of it wrong, and mean none of the errors. The four evaluators were working officers who had read every exhibit they were handed. Ilsev had asked the seat to minute the edge of her own chair, so that nobody could push her past it. At the records table sat a man who had receipted a hundred and seventy-nine leaves without losing one. And Jent, with his travelling inkwell and his mended cuffs, had given away every point that was not true as soon as he was shown it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:35:"In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward," said the seat's clerk. "Upon the close. The twelfth sitting. The petitioner."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:39:He had no folder today. He had left even the one folder on the table, shut, with his spectacles lying on it, and he stood in front of the platform with his hands at his sides, as a man stands who has come to say a thing he could say in his sleep and does not wish to seem to be reading it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:41:"The seat allowed each party an hour," he said. "The petitioner will use ten minutes, and I should like to say why, so that it is not taken for discourtesy. There is very little left between the parties. I have spent a year making sure of that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:45:"The enrollment was lawfully made. The provision under which it was made is law, and has not been repealed, and the seat has held so. The renewals are good upon their days; the respondent's table of them is the petitioner's own. The evaluator of record is competent, and his figures are honest, and his notes were produced entire. The tournament office's record is accepted. And the house has kept within its charter in every particular I have been able to examine, and I examined a great many." He paused. "I want one of those in the minute in my own voice, and not only in a schedule. The Chancellor has done nothing wrong. She answered under escort. She read every order at her own gate. She produced what was asked before it fell due. If every respondent I met were the Chancellor, I should have very little work, and I should think the better of the registry for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:53:"If it does not, the code says what follows. It said so before anybody here was born. The petitioner did not write the fourteenth chapter and does not ask the seat to add a word to it. He asks only whether the registry may put the question. That is a matter of discretion, and I have said so since the fourth sitting, in answer to the best question I was asked all year."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:61:Cael had sent the read after that man five times in the year and found nobody in the coat. He did not send it now, and he found that he no longer wanted to. He had spent months wishing Jent would turn out to be the arm, because an arm could be found out. But Jent was a hand, and a clean one. He had said exactly what he was asking for, in four sentences, before two hundred people, and every one of the four was true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:71:"The registry has ordered frames tested," she said. "It has ordered stations inspected, and records opened, and officers examined upon their conduct. It has ordered practitioners evaluated, a great many of them, where a provision had lapsed, or was wanting, or had been got by a false paper. The house has read every such order it could find." She turned the rule a quarter turn. "It has not found one in which a seat ordered a practitioner into a frame over a provision that stood. Not one. The petitioner was asked whether he had, at the ninth sitting. He answered that he had not, and that he did not need one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:75:"He is right that he does not need one. A discretion needs no precedent. But the seat may like to know, before it uses a tool, that nobody has ever picked it up by that end." The rule ticked again. "That is the house upon the remedy. Upon the merits, the house's close is the work of its advocate of record, who drew every submission this seat has received from it. The house renews its motion under the second paragraph of the hearing provision, and asks that she be heard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:87:"The advocate of record for the respondent will be heard at the next sitting. She will be heard once, and within the hour. The petitioner may reply, if he is so advised, and not otherwise. The seat will then reserve." The level voice went on with no more weight on it than before. "So ordered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:93:On the steps, in a thin bright wind, the counsel stopped with her bag in her hand and looked up at Karis, who was a head taller and looked, just then, about twelve years old.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:107:Cael came along the rim on his way to the wing with a floor sheet he no longer needed to lodge, out of a habit that had outlived its use, and stopped at the rail. The oak lay four feet below him in the hole somebody had dug for it, sixty feet by forty, pale and dry, with the stone tiers going up empty on three sides. On the fourth side the porter's four black boards stood on their wall above the faculty stand, chalked with the year's names. Ephram's was at the head of the Iron column, where it had stood since before Cael came up the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:111:It was the Blade drill he had been running on the day he first called Cael down to the oak, slow, against nobody, with the lath low in his right hand. Heel. Hip. Edge. He did it three times in a row without a pause between, each a different way in, and each began before the last had ended, so that the three made one thing. Then he walked back to his chalk and did it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:121:"I know they are. I ruled the line." Ephram turned then, and came to the edge of the oak under the rail, and stood looking up with the lath across his shoulders and the sweat drying on him. He had a plain open face, and it had never been much use to him for keeping things in. "I'm keeping something," he said. "I'd like you to know I'm keeping it, so that you don't think I've forgotten."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:139:Karis read the slips to the window for three days, and on the fourth night she asked for the key.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:153:"The seat will consider the motion at the close," said the blanket, in Brom's voice, very slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:171:She did not read them. He had thought she would. She laid the thirty slips face up along the edge of Rooke's table in three rows, in their order, and looked at them once, and then she turned her back on them and spoke to a horse blanket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:175:"That is the whole of the respondent's close," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:181:"I know. I could feel it." She had turned back to the table and was already moving slips, and her face was pink. "Everything from the sixth to the eleventh is the renewals and the nine days. He conceded the renewals. He adopted my table; he said so again this morning. I'm proving a thing to a seat that the other side has signed." She took five slips out of the first row and laid them face down. "I liked those. She said they'd be in the part I liked best."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:5:"Call days," she said. "Four of them, before any ruling. In those four days a seat may recall a party to put a question it has thought of late, and so may its evaluation seat, and the code gives them an hour's notice to do it in. An hour, on foot, from wherever the party is lodged." She looked out at the switchbacks. "That was a fair rule when every respondent lived over his own shop in the town. It was not written with a bluff in mind. Your hill is an hour and a quarter from that room by a good horse, and so for four days the house must live where it can hear the exchange's bell. The code calls it keeping within the bell. I have always thought it rather a handsome phrase for a nuisance."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:33:The counsel sat at the table's end with the code open in front of her, the fat volume with the schedules, and a single sheet beside it that was not the house's paper. Withrow sat across from her with both hands on the wood. Bracken stood by the hearth with his back to the room. His spectacles were pushed up on his forehead and he held a slip of paper in his hand, and he looked like a man who had read something off a wall and wished he had not been the one to walk past it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:35:The counsel was speaking. Cael could not hear a word. He saw her lay one finger on a line in the code and then on the sheet, and then on the code again, lower down, as a person does who is adding days. He saw the rule turn its quarter turn at the end of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:45:He told himself it was the ruling they were preparing for, some form to be ready, some order of who should stand where. It was very likely that. But he lay awake a while with Brom breathing across the room like a slow bellows, and he kept seeing her hand come across and close the book. It was the way a person shuts a ledger after reading the sum at the foot of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:49:There was a wool press in the yard behind the guesting-house, and by the second morning Brom and Lira had found a use for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:77:Cael had let the old read go out across the flags without deciding to. It came back to him as it always did, in weight and where the weight went. The platen's whole mass, and a girl's, and the speed she had brought with her, ran down through two forearms into the shoulders, both alike now, and down the long back and through the heels into the stone. The skin had gone dull at the wrists. Brom stood under a wool press like a third post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:101:"I went and looked at the district hall yesterday," she said. "From outside. It's three streets over. It's got a blue door, and a board beside it with the sittings on, and the first one after the year's close has six names. Mine's the fourth. Somebody's written *Wind* after it, and then *Halcenvane*." She picked at the tape on her wrist. "I stood there a good while. A carter asked me if I was lost."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:115:He had told himself all year that whatever this cost him, it should not cost them. Karis had read that over his shoulder and said it was not how any of them were built. Looking down at the two of them at the pump, he understood for the first time what she had meant by it. It was not his to decide what they paid. It never had been. He could only see to it that when the day came, each of them was asked, and not carried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:121:It was three leaves. She had written it at the parlour's long table in the morning, in pencil first, from habit, and then in ink, and the counsel had read it standing and signed the foot without altering a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:123:"It's upon the entry they read out," said Karis, as they crossed the lane. "The certificate. The code lets a seat go on by its own authority where the certificate's withheld; I looked at the section the night we got home. So I went back through the digests to see how often a seat ever had." She held up four fingers. "Four times. I've set them out, with their years. A guild hall's frame, where the assessor wouldn't sign. Two station frames in the hill districts. A plate. Four times in the whole record a seat has proceeded without the certifying office's name, and every time it was upon a thing. Never once upon a person."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:129:The seat's office was a hatch in the exchange's side passage, with a brass grille and a shelf. The junior chair sat behind it. Cael had never seen the man so close. He was a man with a careful face and a clerk's stoop, and he took the three leaves from Karis without lifting his eyes above her hands. He counted them and looked at the signature and entered them in a book against the hour. He wrote a receipt in a plain square hand, and tore it at the rule, and slid it under the grille.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:163:He knew what was in that building. He knew it as he knew the inside of Hesk's shop. There was a room with a brass plate in it. The girl would be told to stand still, and to put her hand where she was shown, and something in her that had been asleep for fourteen years would wake, and the plate would light. In the frame-house a keeper would read what the frame gave him, and write it in his station book in two figures, a reading and a margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:181:He waited. He did not count, for once. The mother sat with the basket and looked at the door. The coat, forty yards back, had stopped by a garden wall and was looking at a row of beans. He had seen a thousand stations, and this one had nothing in it for his column.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:213:It came by the seat's own messenger, a boy in grey with a strap of papers, who would not give his leaf to the woman of the house and stood in the passage until Bracken came down. Bracken read it on the stair with his first pair of glasses and then with his second.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:217:"What does an evaluation seat return?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:235:"The evaluation seat makes its return," she said. "I shall read it at the pace of dictation. I ask that it be taken down as read, and not from my leaf afterward."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:237:"As read," said the clerk, and dipped his pen. At the records table the junior chair wrote the time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:241:"The scope of this seat was minuted at the third sitting, in the presiding seat's words," said Ilsev. She spoke slowly, with a small pause at the end of every clause, and three pens went with her. "It has kept within them. Within that scope the evaluation seat is obliged to lay before the proceedings any matter upon the subject's file that would govern the conduct of an evaluation. There is one such matter. The file carries a designation. The petitioner has set it out in the second schedule to his petition, at the fourteenth line. A designation of that kind governs who must be present at an evaluation, and to what office its result is sent."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:245:"This officer has twice referred that designation, in the proper form, to learn the office that entered it. She has received two returns. She will read both into the record, entire, as they came to her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:249:"The first. *Return, from the chain of review, to the senior evaluation seat, on referral of the matter at Greyvane. Designation: Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.*" The pause. "*Determination: no originating authority of record. Designation predates file creation. No action available at this clearance.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:261:"The evaluation seat notes the contradiction. The registry relies upon a designation to govern an evaluation, and the registry's own returns say that no office of record entered it. The evaluation seat makes no finding upon that. It is not within the scope minuted for this seat. This officer has been told that no further inquiry is authorized, and she makes none. She reads what she was sent." A last pause, no longer than the others. "That is the return."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:273:There had been no file yet. The registry had just said so. It had said so twice, under seal, in a clerk's round hand, and a careful woman with short grey hair had read it out at the pace of dictation in an empty wool exchange so that it would be taken down right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:275:She did not know what it meant. He was as sure of that as he had been of the man at the station door. She had found a hole in the record and been told not to look into it, and she had done the one thing that was left inside her clearance, which was to say aloud, in public, that it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:277:"Received from the evaluation seat," said the clerk, "and entered in the minute. A fair copy will stand in the public record before the ruling. Has either party an observation?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:291:"That's the truest sentence anybody has entered about you in a year of sittings," she said, "and the woman who read it out has no idea what it says."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:11:"You've a minute in hand," said Bracken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:13:"I've a minute I mean to spend being slow." She did not turn her head. "I go too fast when I'm frightened. I've marked the places."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:19:*Advocate of record for the respondent, under the hearing provision.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:21:He had written it at every sitting since the fourth. This morning he wrote it more slowly, and when he had finished he turned the board round on his arm, which he had never done before, so that she could read it the right way up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:33:Ilsev was in the middle chair, reading, and her four evaluators had their pens out. At the records table the senior chair had the minute and Havel had the times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:37:Cael watched him do it. In thirteen sittings the man had worn them to look at the sky and to read a leaf, and had taken them off to speak and to listen alike. He put them on now, and folded his hands on his shut folder, and turned his whole body a little in his chair toward the house's table, as Brom turned toward a frame.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:43:"In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward. Upon the close. The thirteenth sitting. The advocate of record for the respondent, by the seat's leave."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:51:"The house does not ask the seat to find anything the petitioner disputes," she said. "It could not. There is nothing left that he disputes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:55:"I should like to begin by reading the seat a list. It is the list of what is agreed between the parties, and it is the longest such list I have found in any contested proceeding in the digests. I looked, because I wanted to be able to say so truthfully." She did not look down, for there was nothing to look down at. "That the enrollment was lawfully executed. That the provision it was executed under is law. That the renewals are good upon their days. That the evaluator of record is competent in every particular. That every figure he has signed was honestly come by. That the tournament office's record is accurate. That the house has kept within its charter. Seven things. The seat held the first two at the preliminary, and the petitioner gave me the other five himself, across three sittings, without being asked twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:59:"I thanked him for the first of them. I did not thank him for the rest, because my counsel told me not to, and she was right. I thank him now. It is a rare thing to be opposed by somebody who will not keep a point he does not own."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:67:"At the fourth sitting I was allowed one question," she said. "I asked the petitioner what evidence, if any, would defeat the petition. His answer is in the minute, and I have read it most days since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:79:"A discretion is not a blank leaf. Nobody who drafts a code hands an officer a blank leaf. A discretion is a tool, and it is handed over with its purpose written on the handle, and where a code is well made the purpose is written down. In this code it is. The sections under which the petition is brought have a preamble, and the house's counsel read it to this seat at the preliminary. It took her ninety seconds. I shall take less."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:83:"Instruments," said Karis. "That is the handle. I went and read how the tool has been used, to see whether the hands that held it could read, and they could. There are ninety-one recorded invocations of these sections in the registry's whole record. Eighty-four concern things: a frame that read a rank high, a station seam that let in the damp, a plate, a ledger, a seal. The sixty-third was filed by the registrar who is sitting at this table, in his fourth year, upon a station frame in this town, and the registry thanked him for it." Bracken looked at the seal's box. "The other seven name a person, and I have read each of them through. What the registry complained of, every time, was an act: somebody had sent in a false return, or paid for a rank, or lent out a seal. Not one of the seven complains of what the person was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:87:"I am not telling the seat anything the petitioner denies. He was asked at the preliminary whether the use was new, and he said that it was, and struck out a sentence of his own that had suggested otherwise, and the seat minuted the word. *Novel.* It is in the minute of the third sitting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:89:In the row of chairs below the platform one of the four evaluators had stopped writing and was simply listening, with his pen held a little off the page, and Ilsev had not turned a leaf since Karis stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:95:"The petition asks that the enrollee be stood in a frame, under the standard protocol, and it calls that an evaluation. I want to say what an evaluation is. It is a question whose answer is not known beforehand; that is all it is, and it is the only thing that makes it one. When a wool-factor in this room lays a fleece on his scale, he does not know what it weighs. If he knew, he would not weigh it. He would write the figure down and go to his dinner." Somebody at the back laughed, shortly, and was hushed. "A weighing whose result is known before the fleece goes on the pan is not a weighing. It is a ceremony."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:99:"Is the answer known? The registry's own tournament office has entered it, on the record of the continental meet, under a regional registrar's seal. *Unscorable under standard.* That is the registry writing down, in its own book, what its own protocol does with this practitioner."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:103:"The Compact's senior evaluator advised upon the question in three sentences, and the petition quotes them, with approval, at its seventh paragraph. I did not put them there. They say that the enrollment is lawful upon its face, and that if the Compact wishes practitioners of this kind not to compete it should write different rules. *Practitioners of this kind.* Nobody writes that of a man he expects a frame to read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:107:"At the third sitting the evaluation seat asked this seat whether its scope reached the question of whether the standard protocol can read this practitioner at all, and the seat answered that it did not. I make no complaint of the answer. I say only that the question was thought worth asking, by the most careful officer in this room, and that it is minuted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:115:Cael sat very still behind Withrow's chair. He knew what the eleven seconds had been, and so did she. She had read them to him out of a locked case, with a glass, on a night when she wore gloves; and she had just laid them before a seat as a plain fact from a district's record, with nothing behind them, because that was all she was allowed to lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:119:"So this is the remedy," said Karis. "Stand a man on that scale, write down that it returned nothing, and then apply to him the chapter of the code that opens by supposing an evaluation under the standard protocol which *returns no valid classification.*" She gave the four words their full length. "I have read the fourteenth chapter a great many times this year. It is a careful piece of drafting. It was written for the case in which the registry tried, in good faith, to find out what a person was, and could not, and it gives the registry something to do in that case, so that the person is not left in no column at all. It is harsh, and I think it was meant as a kindness."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:125:She stopped, and breathed. He saw her shoulders go down a little, and knew she had come to one of the marked places and was spending her minute there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:127:"Now I come back to the handle. These sections are for the testing of instruments, so that the registry may know which of them to trust, and there are two instruments before the seat. One is a frame, which was asked about this practitioner once and had no answer. The other is twenty-two officers of the registry. The house's counsel read their names to the seat at the preliminary, each with a grade and a house and a day: a provost's panel, a regional registrar's assessors, the tournament's, an evaluator of record who has sat across a trestle from him every week for two years. They watched, and wrote down what they saw, and signed it. Their notes agree with one another, and the petitioner has accepted every one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:131:"Twenty-two officers can see him, and one instrument cannot. The preamble tells the registry what to do when its instruments disagree: it is to find out which of them is to be trusted. It does not say that the registry is to prefer the one that failed, and fine the man for the failure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:139:There was no slip in her hand; the slips were in the wallet on the table, twenty-five in one bundle and five in the other. But he had heard her say the close three times through, and three times she had stopped after this part, and looked at nothing, and gone on. He had counted the pause in the east hall, and it was four seconds long. She stopped now, and looked at the grey shape above her for four seconds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:141:He knew what lay in that pause as well as he knew his own Log. It was the true argument. She had just told a seat, with perfect accuracy, that its instrument had failed to read him, and she knew why it had failed, and what was appended to the reading, and how long it had been so, and whose hands had signed for it. She could have dated the thing to within a renewal of Greyvane's charter. It would have taken her nine minutes, and she had the nine minutes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:151:She told them what the house had made. It was not a boast, and she did not dress it; it was two years of a practitioner's work set down by the people whose office it was to set it down. This year alone there were seventeen panel notes, produced entire, and the leaf nobody had asked for was among them. There were a hundred and seventy-nine exhibits, and not one had been found out of its order; the records table had counted them, she was told, and she was content to rest on the records table's count. At that table the junior chair's pen stopped for the length of a word and went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:153:"The petitioner called it the best-documented enrollment he had reviewed in thirty years, and I believe him. If the remedy is granted, the registry will strike that record. In its place it will enter three words: *no valid classification.* It will exchange the fullest true entry in its books for an empty one, and it will do that under sections whose only purpose is that entries be true."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:157:"I do not say that would be unlawful. I say it is the one use of this discretion that defeats what the discretion is for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:159:Then she spoke of the people who would come after, and she did it quickly, for she had cut it twice. A ruling stood in the registry's books, she said, for every house that held a charter in the same words. The seat had before it four old challenges to the clause, and in one of them a ferryman's daughter in a southern river house had waited a year for a station to settle upon what she was; the house had waited with her, and at the end of the year the station had read her, and she had stayed. Under the ruling now asked for, nobody need have waited. Somebody need only have filed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:165:"The house asks for one thing. It does not ask the seat to hold the petition unlawful. It asks the seat to use the discretion it has for the purpose that is written on it. The entry is true. Twenty-two of the registry's own officers made it. Leave it standing." Her voice did not break, though he had been afraid that it would. "That is the whole of the respondent's close."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:179:"The registry does not dispute a syllable of it," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:183:"I closed a week ago, and I will not close twice. I rise only so that the advocate is not left to wonder whether she was heard. She was. Every proposition she has put to the seat is true. The petitioner adopts her account of what is asked, in her own words, the scale included; I could not have stated my own petition so exactly." He paused. "And it stands where it stood. She has shown the seat that the discretion would be unwisely used, and I think she has shown it beyond answer. But a discretion is not defeated by being shown to be unwise. It is only exercised, or it is not, and that is the seat's and never was mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:189:"The seat has heard the parties," said the hood. "Before it reserves, the clerk will read the standing entries."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:191:The clerk rose with the minute book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:195:The four district evaluators were writing it down. They would have returns of their own to make, and they copied each line as it came, four pens moving together along the row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:199:The clerk read the entry as he had read the others, in the code's own old words, as the form's column was headed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:201:"*Evaluation-protocol certification: declined by the evaluator of record; proceeding on the presiding seat's authority.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:205:It was a small thing to see, and he did not think the benches saw it. The four district evaluators had been writing in a row, and at that line they stopped, one after another, like men on a road who see the one ahead halt. They were working officers. Every one of them had stood practitioners in frames for years, under a protocol that carried the Archmarshal's certificate as a matter of course, as a loaf carries a baker's mark; the certifying office had put its name to that protocol since before any of them was born. It had been asked twice for its name and had not given it, and the form had no line for why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:209:On the fourth bench Gault had not moved. He was the evaluator of record, the only man in that room who was called so every day, and he had declined nothing in his life. He sat with his case straight on his knees and his eyes on the pasted form, and then he took out his small book and wrote a line in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:213:That was the whole of it. Five words had gone out from a desk by a noon dispatch, after a man had weighed them for four days on a post road and found them worth sending though they would stop nothing. They had stopped nothing. They were in the minute as an absence, between the count of the exhibits and the count of the orders, and the clerk was already reading the next line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:249:"It wasn't for the seat," he said. "I thought it was, all year. It's in the minute now. Every word she said is there under her own name, in the registry's own presses, for as long as there's a registry, and somebody will read it. Some girl in a reading room with ink on her collar, looking for something else." He watched Karis's pale fingers, open on her knee. "And it was for her. She wanted to do the thing properly, once, with everybody looking. She's done it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:7:"Junior," said the senior chair. He had the minute open in front of him and was running his thumb down the fresh page. "This wants to be in the porch before the ruling. That's the code, for a return. I've a tooth that won't let me climb, and the clerk's gone to his dinner. Take it up to the loft and stand over them while they copy it. Fair, one sheet, and you compare it yourself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:11:He took the minute book in both hands. It was heavier than it looked, as a book is that has had a year written into it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:19:She began. He stood at her shoulder, as the manual said an officer was to stand when a minute left the records table, and read each line as she made it against the line it came from.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:21:He had not read it yet himself; he had only heard it, and he had always known that those were different things. He had sat forty feet from the middle chair with his pen moving, and had written a time, and had kept his eyes on his own page while a voice he had worked under for years said a thing slowly into an empty room. Now he read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:23:The second of the two returns he had by heart. He had read it once before, upside down, on a desk at Norhold, in perhaps three seconds, and had written it that night at the head of a sheet of his own, which lay in his kit at the inn, the fifth of six. He watched the copyist make the words, and they were the same words, and he felt nothing about them that he had not felt already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:25:The first he had never seen. He had carried the file it answered; he had laid that file at her elbow in a hall on a bluff, square to the edge, and gone back to the foot of the table. A return had gone into it then, and he had not read it, because a records officer did not read what was addressed to the senior seat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:27:*Determination: no originating authority of record.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:35:He stood very still at the copyist's shoulder. It was an answer, and he made himself be exact about that, as he was exact about a count. He had kept six sheets of things the registers could not answer, and here, in a minute, under a seal, was the registry's own hand saying of the first of them that it could not. He had not been wrong, on a pump step, when he was a younger man. He had only been alone with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:43:Havel looked at the line in the minute. It was in the senior chair's hand, taken down at dictation, and the senior chair did not drop words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:45:"It is as it was read," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:49:"It is not required to. A fair copy says what the minute says. If the minute is wrong, that is for the seat, and it will be wrong in the copy too, so that the seat can find it." He heard his own voice, level and dry, saying what the manual said. "Copy it as it stands."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:53:He did not let himself go on to the thing she had seen. He was aware of it, as a man is aware of a stair he has decided not to climb. A girl from a counting-house had read that sentence once and known at a glance that it could not be so; the senior evaluator had read it out in a wool exchange, and called it a contradiction, and made no finding. She had done what her clearance allowed, to its edge, and stopped there. He knew what it was to stop there. He had been doing it for years with a kit bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:61:The exchange's porch had a board for the seat's public record, behind a brass grille that opened with the junior chair's key. He put the sheet up with four pins, and squared it by the grille's bars, and locked it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:63:There was nobody on the steps. A boy came across from the trough, one of the lads the correspondents paid to watch the board, and read the heading with his lips moving: *Return of the evaluation seat.* He looked for a ruling under it and did not find one, and went back to the trough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:75:But it was not the same now, and he made himself see why. She had not taken out the sheet in her coat, which was hers and would stay hers. She had taken out the returns, which were the registry's, and stood up in the registry's own room, and read them into the registry's own minute at a pace a clerk could follow. She had found the one door her clearance left open and had gone through it in the plainest way there was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:85:He read them through, slowly, as an officer reads anything he means to rely on. Then he did something he had not done to them before. He took the pencil from the kit's loop and wrote a small figure in the top corner of each, from one to six. They had never been numbered, for to number a thing was to say how many of it there were to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:87:He did not take a clean sheet. He looked at the place where the clean sheets lay, and thought about what a seventh would say. It would say that on the fourth call day the senior seat read two returns into the minute; and that was in the minute, and pinned up in a porch. The six held what the registers could not answer, or would not, and this the registers had answered themselves, aloud. A thing that stood in the public record did not want a private sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:119:It was true, every word. He read it over once and signed it and dated it for the morning, and laid it on top of the kit, where he would see it when he woke, to hand in at the district seat on his way to the exchange.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:175:The hood read. It read in the voice it had used all year, level and unhurried, with no more weight on one word than on another, and it paused at the end of each sentence so that the clerk might have it. Cael fixed his eyes on the exchange's clock over the doors.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:177:"*The seat, having heard the petition and the responses in full, and having found the enrollment lawful in its execution and the respondent institution compliant with its charter throughout — which findings stand undisturbed — nonetheless finds the evaluation-integrity question sustained on the merits. The demonstration provision describes the practitioner; it does not evaluate him within the meaning of the integrity provisions. The petition for compelled re-evaluation under standard Arbiter protocol is granted. The re-evaluation shall be conducted at the district Arbiter station within five weeks. Should the re-evaluation return no valid classification, the procedure of the code's chapter fourteen shall apply: nullification, suspension, asset-restriction. The demonstration-provision enrollment is revoked effective at the close of the present academic year.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:193:There was no trick in it. A year of Fifth-days had been walking toward exactly this leaf, and every door on the way had been real law and had opened one way, as Karis had told them on the first night. He had read it better than they did, and so had she, and it had made no difference, because it had never turned on the reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:195:What he felt was not the floor going. It was a corridor ending. He had been walking down it so long that he had stopped feeling the walls, and now there was a last door, with a year's close chalked on it, and behind him nothing whatever that he needed to go back for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:197:The seat rose. It gathered its one leaf, and turned, and went to its own door, and for a moment the grey back was all there was of it. He had tried at fourteen sittings to learn something from how that shape sat, and had wanted a face under it, because a face could be read, and hated, and found out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:211:"I heard it read," said Withrow. Her voice was perfectly even. "I shall know where to send him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:215:"Advocate," he said, and that was all. He said it as he had said it into the minute at the fourth sitting, when it had been a courtesy; and it was not one now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:217:Then he looked at Cael, and Cael looked back and did not send the read. He had sat across from this man at a conference table in the year's fourth week and found nobody in the coat, and he knew why now. There had been nobody to find. Jent had been sent with an honest question, and had asked it honestly, and had been given the answer the code gave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:223:"I want one thing said before the hill gets hold of it," she said. "I read losing filings for twenty years at the registry. It was my work to say why they had lost. They lost because a date was wrong, or a section was misread, or somebody had hoped a seat would not notice a thing it was paid to notice." She looked at Karis, and then, longer, at Cael. "You did not lose badly. I should like that to be the first thing either of you remembers about today, and I shall say it to anybody who asks me. There is not a line in that ruling that answers a line of yours. It could not answer them. It went round."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:237:*Ruling given. No finding against the house. The enrollment revoked, from the year's close.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:239:He read it over, and dusted his fingers, and went behind his counter, where the lamps were lit in pairs down the length of the records hall and both his clerks were already bent over the middle table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:245:Seln did not read it, for he had heard it read. He held it up to the lamp by two corners and looked through it, for some time. Then he laid it flat and took Bracken's boxwood rule from its nail, and measured something at the sheet's edge, and measured it again from the other side. He turned the leaf over and ran his thumbnail very lightly round the rim of the seal, as Bracken had once done under an arch, and bent close to the last line to look at the hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:251:"I have read paper all year for what stood behind it," he said. "A mark a sixteenth low. A lot written above the ruled line, with a mark for a word. A seal that is on no schedule. I have been reading paper that way for a good while longer than a year." He touched the leaf's corner. "This is registry stock from the first mould, and the mark sits where it should. The die has its number in the rim, and the number will be on its line in the cutters' ledger; I should be surprised if it were not. The hand is the seat's own clerk's. I have watched him write it since the fortieth day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:255:"It is exactly what it says it is. There is nothing behind it at all." He said it in his mild voice, as he might have reported a floor schedule. "In my trade a man is taught four grades of pressure, in their order. The record. The instrument. The knock, which is paper nobody will own. And last the hand, which is a road with no paper at all. I was taught that the hand was the worst of them, and I believed it for fifteen years." He shook his head, very slightly. "I had the order wrong. What stood in the road was a glove." He looked down at the last line of the leaf. "*Given under the Compact's hand.* That is the only true sentence of its kind I have been shown this year. This is the hand. It does not have another."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:279:He had said, in a harness room, that he could not read a registry sheet anymore; that since the case he looked at one and saw only what it was a form of. He was not reading these. He was doing the one honest thing he could still do with such paper, which was to say what it weighed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:17:The first cut came, heel and hip and edge, high, from the sun. He did not slip it. He put up his left forearm, as a man puts up a guard, and opened the give, and let the edge land where he could read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:31:He cut in earnest. Cael saw the whole engine of it, close, in the moment before it reached him: the heel that committed the body, and the hip that carried it, and the edge that could not then be called back by the man who had sent it. And he saw the other thing, which he had never understood from the rim. The line did not stop at the edge. It ran on, out past the lath's tip, straight as a rule, and Ephram *knew* along it; whatever was going to cross that line, he had word of it a breath before it came. That was how a lath had been lying at the end of a lane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:49:He had only the old read then, and it was enough, because he had stopped waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:69:He had learned. Cael could see it in how the man set his feet. He had learned in three exchanges what a rule of the house had kept from him for three years: that a lane was a line, and could be read; that the push took a heel, if the heel went down slowly; and that the boy in front of him was not quick, but early, so that no chain of cuts would close on a man who knew each link before it was forged.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:71:So he would not chain. Cael read it in his weight before Rooke's hand had moved. Ephram was going to do the simplest thing a Blade could do: he would wait for a moment when there was only one place Cael could be, and then put everything he had into a single cut at that place, shoulders and all, the plain entry from the first year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:85:He laid it out of the corner, down the only gap, fifteen feet along the foot of the wall. The dust woke in the joints and ran, and his ears closed and opened. It was a line a blind man could have read, and Ephram read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:93:He had a breath, perhaps, before the bill. He took the read back off the air and went the other way, a single stride, straight at the man, inside the arc of the cut, to the place where the lath's root would pass at the top of its swing, a foot from Ephram's own shoulder. He set his feet there. He put up his left forearm as a post, and he shut the give.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:178:The clerk looked up at the first board, at the two words Rooke had written there in the dark, and then he sat down at the long table and dipped his pen. He entered the bout twice in the house's book, once under each fighter, as a man does who has been told to get a thing down where nobody can rub it off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:240:"Whatever you are," he said, "I said I'm glad it's on my side of the floor. At the trial. When she stopped to read you." He looked at his bound shoulder. "The floor's about to be the whole continent, and I'm not on it — I'm staying; you know I'm staying; it's the right thing for me and you'd tell me so if I asked, which is why I'm not asking." He put out his left hand. "Still glad. That's the whole thing. I made myself wait two years to fight you straight and it was worth every month, and I don't need to know what you are to know that. Go be it somewhere with more room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:242:Cael took the offered hand with his right, which was the only one he could close, and for a moment neither of them let go. The clerk came down the ladder beside them and went away with his sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:264:*Nine. Taken in earnest from a friend who asked me to show him straight — so I showed him straight, and the architecture did what it does with earnest, and the notice said "non-hostile," which it has never said, because there has never been a fight like it in the log. Ephram asked for the true measurement. He got it. He doesn't know he gave me a piece of himself for it; he consented to a fight, not to the thing the fight fed. I wrote the price down before the bout, the way the covenant says, so that when it cost — and it cost — I'd have written the cost before I collected it, which is the only line between me and what the office did at a road cut and in a records hall — spending people whose names it never bothered to write down, and asking nobody anything.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:268:He read it over once. *And it cost* was true of more than he had priced the night before, and he set that down next, and then the bills, smaller.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:278:*Why not. One: it's on no paper the registry holds, and a man with a pencil had the top of the west tier. Two: it came off somebody who used it to put people in a wagon, and I've not yet found a use for it that isn't a hand on a person. Three: he asked me for a fight, and it holds people. You don't do that to a friend who has asked you for a fight. It stays in the kit.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:296:The sun had gone behind the lecture range. Below him the oak lay in its pit, pale and dry and empty, sixty feet by forty, with a few scuffs on it near the wall where a man had knelt. The porter had finished the tiers and gone, and up on the wall the four boards were going grey. He could still read Rooke's notice on the first, and beneath it the clerk's three chalked lines of the bout's result.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:300:Its door had stood open, and the lamps were lit in pairs. Bracken was at his counter in his shirtsleeves, with all three pairs of glasses about him and a quire of printed forms at his elbow, on the registry's own stock, and Cael had been able to read their heading from the walk: *Withdrawal of an enrollee from a chartered house.* Bracken had counted four sheets off the quire and laid them in a row on the wood, under the lamp. He had squared each of them to the counter's edge with one finger, and then to its neighbour, and set the pen beside them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:29:*Revocation takes effect: at that day's close.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:31:"The ruling says five weeks," she said. "That is the outside of it. The inside is in a section upon notices, which gives a district five days, and in the habits of a district clerk who has never in my time notified a date one day later than the code allowed him. Bracken went to the district hall on our first morning in the town, to look at the room where Brom would stand, and there is a board beside its door. He read on it that the station's frame-house was reserved for the registry's use on the last day of the year." Her voice was quite even. "It was written up before any ruling was given, and I want you to understand that there was nothing crooked in that. A prudent clerk books a frame when he sees that one may be wanted, exactly as Bracken books a coach, and he would have struck it off again without a thought if the seat had gone the other way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:35:"It is five days. An officer will ride up to my gate with the notice tomorrow, or the day after, and it will be addressed as the last one was: to the chancellor, to produce the enrollee. You will still be mine on that day, for the revocation does not bite until its close." She looked down at the four lines. "I read an order aloud at that gate once and declined it, because nobody could tell me whose it was. This one would be a seat's, and I could not decline it. I should have to walk you into that frame-house myself, under the house's seal, or else refuse a seat in writing and let them enter the refusal in my folder."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:41:"She did. She showed me the arithmetic, and then she showed me the section Karis found, and she recommended that I put it to you." Withrow took her hands off the blotter and folded them. "She was right to recommend it, and it was good advice. If the enrollee withdraws before the day, the order has nobody to stand in the frame and no house to produce him, and my folder shows a challenge answered and a house cleared. I have known since the First-day evening that the best thing that could happen to this academy was for you to walk out of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:71:"In the week after the close a clerk will come up our road with a stamp, and he will write here what the ruling says of the respondent institution. *Respondent institution compliant; no finding.* I shall give him tea. I told you a year ago that the best day this folder could have was a clerk writing *sustained*, and I was wrong, for this is better. It is the cleanest remedy column I have seen against a contested entry in twenty years at the registry and all my years in this chair." She took the finger away. "And in Bracken's enrollment book, under your name, there will be five words in his hand. Under three other names as well, I gather, though I have not asked how many and I shall not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:75:"That is what a year of this comes to on paper. An enrollee was challenged. The house answered and was cleared. The enrollee left. Every inspector who opens that case for the next thirty years will start at the grant and turn forward, and that is what he will read, and he will draw the natural conclusion from it. He will think the boy saw which way it was going, and ran, and that the house was well rid of him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:79:"It is. Forms say what happened and never why, and I have spent my life filling them in." Withrow stood up. She was a tall woman, and she stood very straight, and for the first time since the recess she did not look tired. "The record will say you left. I'll spend the rest of my tenure making the record say why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:109:*Spends: the left forearm. He will have to cut at me, and I'll have to take it where I can read it, on the guard, more than once. A Blade's edge isn't the wood's, and I don't believe the give will hold it. Breath. The hip and the shoulder for everything else, because he's asked for all of it and he's to have it. Ten counts for a lane, and ten again. Then whatever the new one does to me afterward. The last asked* There? *forty times an hour for two days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:115:*That's the cost that doesn't heal in a week. Entered at full weight. I've read it over, and I'm still going to do it, if it comes.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:121:"I said I'd know what I was answering," said Brom, when he saw where Cael was looking. "I do. I find I needn't read the question." He took the book and read the leaf through at his own pace, and set his mark at the foot, the B with the bar. "They can lie there, the two of them. I've signed nothing and I've refused nothing. Somebody will find them when the room's cleared and wonder." He gave the book back. "He'll hit you hard. He hits harder than he looks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:123:Karis was still in the reading room, on the floor, with a lantern. She read the leaf twice, and at *I'll price it as if it didn't* she made a small sound through her nose and wrote *K.* under Brom's mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:125:Lira's door was shut and there was no light under it. He knocked, and nobody answered, and after a moment he tore a corner from the back of a schedule and wrote on it against the wall: *Priced. It's in the book, on my sill. Come and read it.* He pushed it under the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:163:She read the leaf standing, by what was left of the lamp. She had read one of these before, in a week of rain, and had written across the bottom of it in capitals that nobody was to go in after him alone. That one had been about a woman on a road who meant to put him in a wagon; it had frightened her, and it had been simple. This one was not simple. She read down it, through the forearm and the lanes, and came to the part where the pen had plainly stopped and started again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:185:He had not waited for the porter, or for morning. Somebody had come to his door at midnight and told him, and he had put on his coat and gone down to the yard in the dark and chalked it himself, before any officer of the house could wake up and wonder whether it was wise. She watched until he climbed down and held the lantern up to read it over, and then rubbed out one word with his cuff and wrote it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:191:Cael read the board at first light, with a stiff neck and three sets of initials under his own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:203:He read the last line twice. Rooke had closed a floor to observers in the year's seventh week, with a chain and a card, and had kept a ledger with *none* in it ever since; and now he had chalked an invitation to a valley.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:213:He had no line to read at his chalk today, and he found that strange. For a year and more he had read one before every bout: what to take late, and what to give, and the figure it was meant to buy. He had the rotation's book in his coat from habit, and at the north wall he took it out and opened it at the day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:223:It was sixty feet by forty, laid so close that a fingernail would not go between the boards, and sunk four feet below the ground, with three short flights of steps, of which he had come down the east one. The stone tiers went up from the rim on three sides, with eleven hundred seats in them and a little over; he had counted them at dawn in his first week on the hill, when they were empty, and had felt them on the back of his neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:225:They were not empty now. He counted by tens and by rows, as he had counted benches in a wool exchange, and gave it up at twelve hundred, where the stone ran out and the people did not. They stood along the rim behind the top tier, and sat on the steps, and a row of first-years had got onto the wall of the lecture range and were dangling their legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:251:Cael knew the walk. He had stood in front of it for a season on this floor, calling the weight aloud: every angle, every pace, the lath low and never thrown. The old read went out through his boots into sprung oak and came back with Ephram in it, early, as it always did. The weight was forward and a little left, the shoulders had not dropped, and the back heel was down. It was the front foot only, then, and the thing to do was wait.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:259:And the third was coming out of the second. He had watched this from the rim on a First-day, slow, against nobody: three in a row, each a different way in, each begun before the last had ended. He read the heel go down for the third, and the weight, and where the cut would finish, and he knew, a half beat early, exactly where he needed to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:293:Cael stood on the south chalk with the sun on his back, and he could feel every one of them above him. He had felt eleven hundred empty seats on his neck at dawn in his first week, and it was nothing to this. Withrow sat in the registrar's chair, and Gault had signed two years of his figures, and a coat held a pencil at the top of the west tier. And there were four hundred people he had eaten beside since he came up the road, who knew him as a quiet enrollee with a provision and a decent read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:305:The Wind first, because it was the oldest: low and flat off the left foot, straight down the middle of the floor. The oak took the landing and threw him on, as it was built to, and he was in the air again before the beat had finished. Three. Ephram's hands had come off the grip, and he had the lath up, and his heel was going down, and the read had all of it, deep, to the hips, a full beat before it happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:313:Four. He went past the wide cut on the outside, and Ephram turned on the miss faster than a man of his size ought to turn. A line was already there. He had not cut it; he had turned his wrist, and the lath lay level across the place where Cael's next landing would come down, because he read lines, and knew what would cross one a breath before it crossed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:325:The read had gone out into the lane, as it must. He took it back off the air and gave it to the man, as he had learned to at a price, and then he stepped into the downhill and it carried him a third faster than any feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:329:He saw it with a beat to spare, and understood it in the same instant. A lane was a line. It was the longest, plainest line anybody had ever laid on that oak, with dust running down it to show the way, and Ephram had not needed to know what it was. He had read where it went, and had put his edge at the end of it and stood there, waiting, with his heel down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:343:*Six. Seven.* The third was the short one, with no hip in it. He saw the heel go down, and he knew, and he could do nothing with the knowing for a whole slow beat. He spent a burst he could not afford. Seven. It was one past what the oak gave free, and it took him out sideways along the rim wall with the lath's wind on his neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:13:"The ruling," said Karis. "Everybody heard it, and I've the engrossment by heart, so I'll give it once and short. Lawful. Compliant. Revoked from the year's close. A frame within five weeks, on a date they'll send, and the fourteenth chapter when the frame returns nothing." She laid her pale fingers on the three slips. "That leaves three roads, and I've written one on each. I haven't put them in order of anything, because I didn't want to cheat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:35:"I stand in it. Within five weeks, at the district station, with Ilsev seeing to the conduct of it, and she'll see to it properly. I put my hand where I'm shown. It was asked once before, and it took eleven seconds." He did not look at Seln, and he went no nearer to it than that. "We know what it says. Karis told a seat so for fifty-one minutes and nobody contradicted her: *no valid classification.* Then the chapter opens, in its order. One: every enrollment or standing that rests on my being a practitioner is void, and that's this hill. Two: I'm suspended as a practitioner pending a review, and the review is to be begun by the registry at its discretion."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:39:"With no date. Withrow underlined that twice in her own code, nine years apart. And three is part nine." He had it by heart, having heard Bracken read it, flat, on the night of the procedure. "They may restrict, freeze or hold anything that attaches to me: a record, a sponsorship, a travel paper, *any institutional relationship.* They've walked those fences already. They read Lira's file for completeness, and they wrote to Brom's family's estate, and they queried Karis's standing twice, and they sent two polite men to Denvash to ask about a shop's title."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:63:"We have the thing that ends it," said Karis. "It's between Seln's feet. I spent fifty-one minutes last week telling a seat that its instrument had failed to read him, and I couldn't say why. I know why. I can date it to within a renewal of a charter. That isn't an argument from discretion; it's the newest matter anybody has put before the registry in two hundred years, and the code gives an appeal from a seat's ruling upon new matter. I looked it up on the night we came home from the close, because I couldn't sleep. It goes to the convening, within thirty days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:67:"I'm arguing. Let me." Karis held up a finger. "It needn't be the convening. There's the Archmarshal's office. He has read it. He stood at a window in a borrowed room and said what it was, and he has since refused the protocol his name, and a finding from him, with the case behind it, is a thing no seat could set aside as a forgery. And if not him, there's the press. There's a man in the market street who bought a second press this year on the strength of our name, and a Gold at Auremont who has already told the registry in public to classify its instruments. Forty families could be told what was done to their children. It's true, and it's proved, and in my hands it's dated." Her voice had risen a little. "I have never in my life held a true thing and not filed it. I don't know how. Everything I am says that you put it in front of somebody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:79:"An appeal upon new matter is examined before it is heard. That is the code also. It goes to a records office to be verified, and the office would open a district's station book against a clipped order, and find that the two disagree, and report an irregularity in the *exhibit*." He said it without any heat. "Every instrument they would use to test it runs on the thing it accuses. A man told you so at a window. It was his first refusal, and he gave it before any of us had asked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:91:"And the forty," he said. "The families. Say it reached them. A man in a river town opens a sheet and reads that his daughter wasn't entered as her plate showed, eleven years ago, because of an order. What does he do with it? Whose door does he go to?" He lifted his head. "The same door. There's one door, and it's theirs. He takes it to the district, and a clerk who's never heard of any of it looks in the book, and the book says Copper, and the clerk's telling the truth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:93:"It would read as manufactured," said Lira. "You said that yourself, Karis, in a coach, about a road with six people on it and bruises to show. This is paper. An unclassified enrollee loses at a seat on the Fifth-day, and on the Sixth he turns up with a box of old orders that say the whole registry's crooked." She shook her head. "I wouldn't believe us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:103:"Yes," she said. "That's the answer. I knew it was, and I needed to hear it lose." She put her hand flat on the second slip. "You don't file a grievance with the instrument that forged the record. You leave its jurisdiction."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:127:"I do not ask what the room is being used for," he said. "I ask when a thing is wanted, and never why, and I have found that it saves me a great deal. I have come about the district's evening bag, which reached my counter a quarter of an hour ago with one item in it. It is addressed to the practitioner, care of the registrar, and so I have read it." He held out the packet. "I walked his docket up to a refectory table on the fourth day of the year. I thought I had better finish as I began."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:131:There were two papers in it. The first was a full sheet under the district's seal, and Brom read it standing, without moving his lips, and then aloud, slowly, testing each plank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:133:"*Ostrand district registry. Review for advancement of tier: Brom, enrolled at Halcenvane. The panel, having read the record and the house's sheets, and having taken a reading in the sigil frame under load, finds for the tier. Iron. Rank to be entered upon the practitioner's signature before the registrar of his house, within fourteen days.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:139:Brom did not answer, for he was looking at the second paper. It was a printed slip, a third the size of the sheet, with a narrow margin and a rule at its head, and Cael, from the bench, could read its heading upside down: *Guidance to the practitioner upon advancement.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:141:"It is issued with every finding," said Bracken. His voice had changed; it had gone careful. "By rule. A district clerk takes one from a pigeonhole and puts it in the packet. I issued it myself when I was a clerk at that seat, eleven times, and I thought it a kindness, since it tells a young person what the new standing will ask of him. Mostly it is about fees." He took the glasses off his nose and held them. "There is a paragraph near the foot, however, which the clerk is to mark in the margin where a practitioner has associations of record. I see that he has marked it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:143:Brom read it, and this time he did not read it aloud. He read it twice, by the movement of his eyes, and then he held it out across the table, not to Cael but to Karis, because she was the one who read such things, and she read it to the room in her small dry voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:145:"*The practitioner is advised that associations of record with any person subject to an order under the fourteenth chapter may be noted upon the practitioner's file, at the registry's discretion, and may be considered upon any later review of standing or sponsorship. The practitioner may wish to regularize such associations before signature.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:157:He folded the finding along its folds and laid it on the table. He did not sign it, and he did not tear it, and he did not say that he refused it. He put it down as a man sets down a thing he has decided not to carry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:175:"*Or to her order*," he said, and his voice was not quite steady. "I wrote that in a deposit book on the first day of the month. I have been thirteen years at that counter and I knew what I was writing." He put his lantern down on the top stair, carefully, and took the last pair of glasses off his forehead, and then there was nothing between his eyes and the lamp. They were wet, and he let them be wet. He stood on the landing with three pairs of spectacles in his hands and wept, briefly and without any noise, as a man does who has kept true records all his life and has just watched one do what this one had done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:193:"I've got it written. I did it on the stair in the records hall, watching him square copies, and it's under *ME*." She read it as she would have read a bout sheet, quickly, with her finger under the line. "The program. That's a hall painted for me, and a red book from a river academy, and a Stone on loan who writes two lines to my coach every week. A year of it. The trial rule, and twenty-seven on a card, and then twenty-seven under a hall's seal at the confluence, against a Silver who told me afterward to go on my fifth. Twenty-eight on the oak against a Blade. Four losses on my sheet, and I know what every one bought." She turned the leaf. "A sitting. The first after the year's close, behind a blue door, with my name fourth on the board. Rooke chalked it over the week's drills and ruled a line under it so nobody could wipe it off. He's been building toward that door since the recess before this year, and he never once asked me to promise I'd walk through it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:205:"I've wanted it all year. I thought the thing I wanted lived on that floor, and I've looked for it there every dawn since the first week." She put the book away inside her coat. "Turns out it doesn't live here. It came up the road with me. I landed on a capstan bar in a wool yard two days ago on whichever foot I liked, with nobody holding a card, and it was all there. It's in my feet. They can't keep it in a hall, and they can't revoke it." She looked at the last slip. "But the ladder lives here, and I'm leaving the ladder. I want that written at full price, and I don't want anybody telling me it's cheap because I can still jump."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:207:He wrote it. *The program, the sitting, Rooke. Full price. It doesn't live here.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:215:"I didn't know how else to do it," she said. "So I've done it as a bibliography, by head-word." And she read it, quite steadily, as she would have read the contents leaf of a bundle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:249:"I know." He looked at his own column, at *Wanted it true*, and set the pen under it. "The hill. Two years, near enough three, of the only place that ever let me in by its own rule. Gault's trestle. A sill. Withrow." He wrote as he spoke. "Whatever part nine does when I'm not there to stand under it. Hesk's shop has no interest of mine recorded in it, but they've asked, and they'll ask again, and I can't be in Denvash to see what they do. A rematch on a floor I can't name yet. A man who said *find me later*." He stopped. "And the first thing I wrote this year. I believed it, and I'm not ashamed that I did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:277:He took that in. He looked at the gaps in the shelves, and at the one slip on the cloth, which he could read from where he stood, and Cael saw him read it. His plain open face did what it had done at a rail in wing three, and went still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:291:He untied the green tape. He took out the three sheets, close-ruled, soft at the folds, and unfolded them on the bare cloth where Karis's slips had been: ninety-eight entries in a square hand, with a dot beside each, and the line of dots breathing down three pages and never once touching the mark ruled across the top in red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:295:There were two lines on the back, in pencil, which he had written at a rail, facing a wall, on the day the hours were complete. He turned the sheet round so that Cael could read them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:303:"I told you on that oak, at the end of your first year, that I'd learn what you were the only honest way there is," said Ephram. "With a floor, and somebody at the rail. I've had ninety-eight lessons since in what you'll let a table see. I've a chart of them, and it's the best-kept record of a thing not happening that anybody ever made." His voice was quite level. "So here's what I've been keeping. I want one bout. The Crown yard, tomorrow, under Rooke's rule, in front of whoever comes. And I want all of it. Not the ceiling. I ruled that ceiling, and I know to the half point what's under it. I want whatever's over it." He put two fingers on the red line. "If you give me the band I shall put my lath down and stand there till the glass runs out, in front of the whole house, and you can explain it to them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:329:"Tonight," said Cael. "On paper, before I sleep. You'll all three have it to read."

