editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:11:*Only inside a fight the other begins, in earnest, awake, and on purpose.* Ephram had begun it in a reading room at midnight, with five people listening, and he had grounded his lath in front of twelve hundred rather than be given less than all of it. Nobody had ever been more in earnest who did not also wish him harm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:65:He did not know when they had stood. He heard them now as he walked back to his chalk, a noise like the weir below Ostrand bridge, and under it somewhere the Stone's enormous voice saying one word over and over. He did not look up. His left forearm had four lines across it, and he could not have closed the hand to save his life; his shoulder had counted one push, and his hip was done with him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:160:The middle of it his arm could have told him. *Iron*: it was the lightest thing he had been handed since before Norhold, and Karis's curve had been right again, for it had come in a breath. *Edge-declaration* was the edge that stood clear of the wood because a man said so, and *line-reading* the thin bright rule that had ended on his third button. *Contact range.* He had five lines across his forearm that said the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:200:He sat on the lowest step of the east flight while it did, because the healer had told him to sit somewhere and hold his arm above his heart. She had looked at the forearm and whistled through her teeth, and felt along both bones, and said that nothing was broken and that it would be the colour of a plum by morning and stay so for a fortnight. She had bound it, and then she had looked at the way he was sitting and asked which hip. He had told her, and she had said that she supposed there was a shoulder as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:214:She did not come to the steps. She looked at the oak, the whole length of it, as a woman looks at a room she has kept for a long time, and up at the first board, where four lines stood in a coach's capitals. Then she looked at Cael on his step, with his arm bound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:240:"Whatever you are," he said, "I said I'm glad it's on my side of the floor. At the trial. When she stopped to read you." He looked at his bound shoulder. "The floor's about to be the whole continent, and I'm not on it — I'm staying; you know I'm staying; it's the right thing for me and you'd tell me so if I asked, which is why I'm not asking." He put out his left hand. "Still glad. That's the whole thing. I made myself wait two years to fight you straight and it was worth every month, and I don't need to know what you are to know that. Go be it somewhere with more room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:272:*Left forearm, wrist to elbow: the touch, and four more on the guard, the last to the bone or near it. Hip, crest to knee: ten on the oak, four past the six. Shoulder: six pushes at load, one past Norhold. Two lanes, ten and ten. Behind the eyes. One spark. Not the quiet thing; in a yard with twelve hundred people looking down it had nothing to offer and didn't. Not the one from the road.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:300:Its door had stood open, and the lamps were lit in pairs. Bracken was at his counter in his shirtsleeves, with all three pairs of glasses about him and a quire of printed forms at his elbow, on the registry's own stock, and Cael had been able to read their heading from the walk: *Withdrawal of an enrollee from a chartered house.* Bracken had counted four sheets off the quire and laid them in a row on the wood, under the lamp. He had squared each of them to the counter's edge with one finger, and then to its neighbour, and set the pen beside them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:304:Seln had looked round a reading room at the top of a library once, on the evening of a birthday, with six people in it and a honey cake. *It is a good room*, he had said. *I have been in a great many.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:25:*That's a decision too. I'm entering it as one. Not deciding, every day, on purpose, by all five of us, with a date on it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:51:"With the registrar, a fortnight ago. Under her name, with the stamp."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:91:"The merits are concluded. The seat sets the matter down for closing." The unhurried voice went on, with no more weight on these words than on any others it had spoken that year. "Two closing sittings, in the last month of the academic year, a week apart. The ruling will be given upon the last Fifth-day of that month. The parties will be notified of the hours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:125:It was a plain room, like all the rooms on that corridor, with a bed and a press and a table under the window, and it was tidier than Cael's because Brom was. The two boxes were under the bed, roped, where they had been since the week of the gate, and Cael had stopped seeing them. The plaque, dark wood and a brass plate with the bar and ring, lay on the shelf on its face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:135:Brom held it for a moment in both hands, and then he laid it on the table, by itself, a hand's breadth from the stack and a hand's breadth from the edge, with the seal uppermost. He put one thick finger on each of its near corners and turned it a very little, until its side lay true with the table's. He looked at it, and moved it the width of a nail, and looked again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:157:On the table under the window the envelope lay square to the edge, with its red seal up, in the last of the afternoon's light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:163:The year's last month opened on a First-day, and Bracken turned his card.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:189:This morning there were three boxes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:199:Three. He counted them twice, which was once more than three boxes needed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:205:*Deposited for safe keeping, the first day of the year's last month. Three boxes, roped. Depositor: K. Dellenmoor. Contents: the depositor's own papers. To be released to the depositor, or to her order.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:243:None of them had said they were leaving. He did not think any of them had decided it, in so many words, even alone. It was only that there were now three boxes on a shelf, and two under a bed, and a carter's book in a coat, and a new strap on a satchel, and five beans in a gardener's jar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:247:*First-day. The year's last month.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:249:*Eight, unchanged. The new one idle; it wants thresholds and takes names instead. The quiet one sealed, on his terms, paid for every morning. Under the eight, the still place, uncounted.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:251:*The seat: two closing sittings and a ruling, the last on the month's last Fifth-day. Leave for Karis to be heard is "to be considered at the close." Brom's panel sits the same day, at the second bell, three streets off.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:253:*On Brom's table: one envelope, sealed, square to the edge. Four weeks now, less a few days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:107:*Posting sought.* The district seat's records room was a man short. He had seen it for himself when he went there with a rubbing of a seal: an impression book four months behind, and a ledger of dies that nobody had balanced against the cutters' copies since the last officer left. He wrote *District records, Ostrand, or any records room that is short.* He did not write *out of monitoring*, for the form had no line for what a man wished to leave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:141:Then Lira came round the corner at something that was nearly a run, and stopped herself, and walked, and Brom came after her at his own pace. He looked just as he had looked going in, with his coat buttoned, except that there were two red bars across his forearms where the cuffs had ridden up, darker than the wool press had left them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:21:"Then you should have the whole of it," said Withrow, "since you have brought me yours." She took a folded sheet from inside her coat and opened it on the blotter and turned it toward him. It was not the house's paper. There were four lines on it in the small capitals of the house's counsel, and each had a day written against it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:35:"It is five days. An officer will ride up to my gate with the notice tomorrow, or the day after, and it will be addressed as the last one was: to the chancellor, to produce the enrollee. You will still be mine on that day, for the revocation does not bite until its close." She looked down at the four lines. "I read an order aloud at that gate once and declined it, because nobody could tell me whose it was. This one would be a seat's, and I could not decline it. I should have to walk you into that frame-house myself, under the house's seal, or else refuse a seat in writing and let them enter the refusal in my folder."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:87:"No. I said in the term that I'd be annoyed if you thanked me, and I should be." She had her hand on the door. "The captain came to me at supper to ask for the Crown yard tomorrow at the fourth bell. I told him the yard was Rooke's to give and the bout was yours, and I see by your face that he has since asked you. I shall be in the stand. I have sat under a rug in other people's halls for two years to watch my own fighters, and I should like to see one thing on my own oak before the year closes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:149:She was going to walk out of this room standing up, and nobody was putting her out. The door was open, and there was a sitting with her name fourth on a board, and a coach who had ruled a line under it so that it could not be wiped off. She could stay. She had turned that over on a stair in the records hall, honestly, watching Brom weigh paper: she could let four people go east, and be at a blue door in a fortnight in clean wraps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:193:He had come down by the rim, alone, before the first bell. The oak lay below him in its pit, pale and dry, and on the wall above the faculty stand the first of the four black boards had been wiped of the year's afternoons, every one, and four lines stood on it in Rooke's square capitals.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:297:Five hands had gone up for this, and he had priced it. He looked down the length of the oak at a man leaning on a lath with the sun in his eyes, who had ruled a red line in the first month he knew him and had waited three years to be shown the far side of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:7:In the coach she sat with the wallet on her knees and did not open it. She had the twenty-five slips by heart; he had heard her say them through twice more since the east hall, once to the reading room's window and once, at midnight, to the landing, when she thought the stair was empty. She looked out at the switchbacks going by, and her fingers lay on the leather, quite still, and paler than the rest of her hand, as they would be for the rest of her life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:215:Cael found that it was enough, and it surprised him. He had wanted a wall, a month ago, and had been given a blank on a form. But he had watched Gault hand up a leaf because it was there, and he had heard Karis spend fifty-one minutes on a room that could not be moved, and he thought he was beginning to learn how the honest ones did it. They did not do it to win. They did the thing that was theirs to do, to its full weight, and let it be entered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:9:Karis had the table's end. Lira sat on the bench with her back to the wall and one boot up, and Brom was in the good chair from the dairy, which had somehow followed them up four flights months ago and never gone down. Seln came last, on the tenth bell, and shut the door behind him without a sound. He had his post satchel over his shoulder, and when he sat he did not hang it on the chair but set it on the floor between his feet, upright, and Cael heard the weight of what was in it meet the boards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:93:"It would read as manufactured," said Lira. "You said that yourself, Karis, in a coach, about a road with six people on it and bruises to show. This is paper. An unclassified enrollee loses at a seat on the Fifth-day, and on the Sixth he turns up with a box of old orders that say the whole registry's crooked." She shook her head. "I wouldn't believe us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:115:"Mine's on my table," said Brom. "Under a whetstone, and beside it. I can tell you what it is without fetching it." He rubbed his forearms, where the red bars were. "There's a finding coming by the bag. I stood for it this morning and I'm not sorry. And there's an envelope that I said I'd know how to answer once the ruling was in. Well, the ruling's in. So—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:167:"Then I'm carrying three boxes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:175:"*Or to her order*," he said, and his voice was not quite steady. "I wrote that in a deposit book on the first day of the month. I have been thirteen years at that counter and I knew what I was writing." He put his lantern down on the top stair, carefully, and took the last pair of glasses off his forehead, and then there was nothing between his eyes and the lamp. They were wet, and he let them be wet. He stood on the landing with three pairs of spectacles in his hands and wept, briefly and without any noise, as a man does who has kept true records all his life and has just watched one do what this one had done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:183:"Then it is a matter of forms, and forms are my trade." Bracken picked up his lantern. "The registry prints one for an enrollee's withdrawal, and I hold a quire of it. In thirteen years I have not had occasion to square more than one at a time on that counter." He looked at the four of them who were enrolled, one after another, and did not look at Seln, who was staff and outside any form of his. "I shall have them ready. I say nothing about how many."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:225:"*Greyvane, the archive at: a second request, sealed by the fee office in the week the first was paid, for the founders' correspondence; answer expected in about eight weeks. I can't forfeit an answer that hasn't come, and so I have written to redirect it.*" She looked up from the sheet, at Cael, and for the first time that night she seemed unsure of herself. "*Care of an instrument-maker, at Denvash.* I ought to have asked you. There wasn't anybody else whose address will still be an address."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:307:"I told you at the trial that whatever you are, I was glad it was on my side of the floor," said Ephram. "I said some hard things on a tier in your first months here, and I've been glad every day since that I was wrong. I'd like to find out whether I'm still glad when you stop being careful with me. I think I will be. I'd like to know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:27:Two hundred and six people, and not one of them was dishonest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:61:Cael had sent the read after that man five times in the year and found nobody in the coat. He did not send it now, and he found that he no longer wanted to. He had spent months wishing Jent would turn out to be the arm, because an arm could be found out. But Jent was a hand, and a clean one. He had said exactly what he was asking for, in four sentences, before two hundred people, and every one of the four was true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:129:He found that he was not afraid of it. A month ago he would have been. He had stood at a rail in wing three and watched Ephram write on the back of a sheet, and had gone cold, because for two years the worst thing anybody on the hill could do to him was to ask. Now there was a date he could count to, and after it there would be either no band or a door, and in neither case anything left to guard from a friend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:23:"Everybody is." Brom laid his whetstone in its sock beside his razor. He had not brought the envelope; it was on his table on the hill, square to the edge, with its seal up. He had brought the summons and the two dockets and the schedule, and they were in the left pocket, and his hand went there now and came away. "Four days. I never thought I'd want a Fifth-day to hurry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:89:He sat down on the pump trough with his forearms on his knees. There were two red bars across them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:113:And there were two boxes roped under a bed, and a carter's book in a coat.

